**(a) No.** Among 14 consecutive integers exactly 7 are odd; call them $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_7$ with $a_{i+1} = a_i + 2$. They must each be divisible by one of $3, 5, 7, 11$. Since $2$ is invertible modulo each odd prime $p$, the residues of $a_1, a_2, \ldots$ modulo $p$ repeat with period exactly $p$, so among $a_1, \ldots, a_7$:

- at most $3$ are multiples of $3$, and exactly $3$ only if they are $a_1, a_4, a_7$;
- at most $2$ are multiples of $5$, and exactly $2$ only if they are $a_1, a_6$ or $a_2, a_7$;
- at most $1$ is a multiple of $7$, and at most $1$ of $11$.

That is at most $3 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 7$ "hits", so covering all seven numbers needs every bound to be attained with no number hit twice. But three multiples of $3$ means $a_1, a_4, a_7$, and then both possible pairs of multiples of $5$ contain $a_1$ or $a_7$, a number hit twice. Contradiction.

**(b) Yes.** Choose $N$ with
$$N \equiv 0 \ (2),\quad N+1 \equiv 0\ (3),\quad N+5 \equiv 0\ (5),\quad N + 3 \equiv 0\ (7),\quad N+9 \equiv 0\ (11),\quad N+11\equiv 0\ (13),$$
possible by the Chinese Remainder Theorem; $N = 9440$ works. In $N, N+1, \ldots, N+20$ the even numbers are divisible by $2$, and the ten odd ones are covered:
- by $3$: $N+1, N+7, N+13, N+19$ (i.e. $9441, 9447, 9453, 9459$);
- by $5$: $N+5, N+15$ ($9445, 9455$);
- by $7$: $N+3, N+17$ ($9443, 9457$);
- by $11$: $N+9 = 9449 = 11\cdot 859$; by $13$: $N+11 = 9451 = 13 \cdot 727$.

So $9440, \ldots, 9460$ are 21 consecutive integers with the property. ∎
